{
  "dom_id": "step-e532",
  "name": "Example: Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/299365e1a9d092be299966bd49858b2245b86dad846e67ae2873b30ea0bd6ac9.png)\n\nSketch the slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 4x+2$ at the points indicated in the diagram above.",
  "worked_solution": "We have to sketch the slope fields at the points $(-1,-2), (0,-2),$ and $(0,1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-2)}=4(-1)+2=-2 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-2)}=4(0)+2=2 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)}=4(0)+2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/617673605e9f13423851496fcfc79d7d60bda5c0569438943ca303c812b68885.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
